just last September Nvidia released the  GTX 980 the most powerful single GPU on  the market and yet here we are just over  six months later and the company has  given us a new piece of sexy silicon to  lust over but as I noticed in my gtx 980  review that card wasn't the generational  leap in performance you might have  expected enter the gtx titan x a full  fat implementation of invidious power  efficient max or architecture that pairs  are wildly over-the-top twelve gigabytes  of vram with seven teraflops of  processing power it's big its bold and  it's badass but boy is it gonna cost you  so just what you get with the titan ex  headline specs of 3072 cuda cores 96  ROPS and 12 gigabytes of vram tied to a  full fat 384 bit memory system and yes  970 owners before you ask that's a full  12 gigabytes at maximum speed compared  to the gtx 980 the Titan X is clocked  slower by few hundred megahertz but the  vastly higher amounts of CUDA cores and  ROPS more than makes up the difference  and hey if you're into overclocking the  Titan X is 6 + 2 phase design means that  you can crank up the power target to 110  percent pushing the wattage up from 250  to 275 watts yes there's a higher TDP  this time around up from 165 watts of  the GTX 980 unlike AMD's cards though  Nvidia is extremely power efficient  architecture has given the company space  to increase performance without  dramatically increasing TDP and heat or  needing a particularly exotic cooling  solution still you'll need six and eight  pin power from your power supply this  time with nvidia recommending a minimum  of  600 watts to get the job done aside from  the snazzy black exterior the Tison ex  uses the same excellent reference cooler  as Nvidia's other cards however unlike  the gtx 980  nvidia is only allowing partners to  release cards with the reference cooler  so don't expect the likes of MSI's  excellent twin throws a cooling system  to be hitting titans anytime soon  not that it matters too much in testing  the GTX Titan is pretty quiet  although thanks to increased wattage it  is noticeably louder than the 980 under  load it's not overly distracting by any  means and it's certainly quieter than  AMD's cards but it's something to bear  in mind if noise is a big issue for you  onto performance then and no surprises  here the GTX Titan X is the fastest  single GPU I've ever tested across the  board there's between a twenty-five and  thirty percent boost in performance over  the 980 and up to 40 percent over an AMD  r9 290x at 1440p the Tizen X pushed  above 60 frames per second in all the  games we tested including yes Crysis 3  going to 4k still showed impressive  results and with the exception of Crysis  3 this time around no game dropped below  40 frames per second the Titan X is the  first card where you can reasonably play  in 4k without having to go with an SLI  or crossfire setup so as expected the  Titan X is now the fastest GPU on the  block and while no GPU is ever really  future proof with 12 gigabytes of vram  onboard it's likely that you'll be able  to keep the Titan X around a while  longer and your average graphics card  and really that's who the Titan X is for  the guy or gal who won't accept anything  less than the very best in performance  this is an enthusiast grade graphics  card with enthusiast pricing to match  but even if you don't take into account  all the great Nvidia extras you get for  the cash g-sync GeForce experience and  game works the Titan X is finally a  generational leap of performance over  the 7 Series and the absolute best  graphics card money can buy
 

